  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply 1 is:\n```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"a\":2}\n```\n{\"a\":3}\nReply 2 is:\n{\"a\":3}\nFor Reply 1, the code fence holds two objects, so the rule requires the last one inside the fence, {\"a\":2}. For Reply 2, there is no fence, so the rule requires the last top-level object, {\"a\":3}. These are different answers. I conjecture the check gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"a\":3}. The reason is that the fenced pool takes the whole fence body as one candidate, '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"a\":2}', which fails strict JSON and last-wins parsing, so the fenced pool yields nothing; the top-level pool then scans the whole text and finds {\"a\":3} as the last top-level object. The conjecture would be false if recover_json_object on Reply 1 returns {\"a\":2} instead of {\"a\":3}, or if the rule is read as allowing the top-level fallback to override a fence that holds multiple objects."
